As more of the human genome is understood, more conditions can be tested for, and the questions grow with them. The specification asks you to weigh four:

• **who decides** who is tested;
• the **risk of amniocentesis** against the safety of a blood test;
• the **dilemma for a carrier** after a test finds a problem;
• whether others, such as **insurance companies**, should see the result.

An ethical reason is about right and wrong, or about who has the right to decide or to know.

• give the sex chromosomes of a female, a male, a germ cell and a sperm, and show why the chance of a boy is 50%;
• say how cystic fibrosis, Huntington's disease and Down's syndrome are caused, and which genotypes have each;
• work out the chances for the children of two carriers, and give the chromosome numbers with Down's syndrome;
• at Higher, follow haemophilia through a family on the X chromosome;
• describe amniocentesis, compare it with a blood test, and argue the ethics of screening.
